          "distinct": true,
          "id": "fa8d800a282cea684f3dda48daa1ab23cdf76634",
          "message": "feat: Prove impossible segments from statistics before scorer creation (#6346)\n\n## What\n\nUse segment statistics to skip impossible segments before creating their\nscorers, and omit the internally added range-partition filter when\nstatistics show every candidate segment already satisfies it.\n\n## Why\n\nSecond of three PRs for #6078: statistics snapshot (#6345) → static\nproofs (this PR) → dynamic pruning (#6347).\n\nThe execution reader already knows which segments are visible. Their\nstatistics can also tell us when searching a segment cannot produce a\nmatch. This PR adds that decision to the existing reader/scorer path.\n\nFor a segment whose `price` statistics are `min=20`, `max=50`, and\n`nullable=false`:\n\n| Predicate | `can_match` | `matches_all` | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `price < 10` | false | false | No row can match; skip the segment. |\n| `price = 35` | true | false | Min/max cannot tell whether 35 occurs;\nevaluate the query. |\n| `price BETWEEN 10 AND 60` | true | true | Every row satisfies this\npredicate. |\n\n`can_match` is the only answer that skips a segment. `matches_all`\nexists for `must_not` clauses and for omitting the range-partition\nfilter; user predicates are never simplified.\n\n## Execution flow\n\n```text\nOpen execution reader using #6345's frozen statistics snapshot\n  → each search asks `can_match(segment, query)` for the segment it is about to open\n  → scorers are created for candidates only\n  → original query evaluates the surviving segments\n```\n\nCandidates are decided for the execution view, including when a prepared\nquery runs after inserts or merges. The full `Searcher` remains intact,\npreserving global scoring statistics.\n\n## How\n\n- **One predicate:** `can_match(segment, query)` and\n`matches_all(segment, query)` recurse over the query for one segment,\nusing its min/max/nullability, and stop at the first impossible `must`\nclause. No truth tables, no decoded-field cache, and no stored\ndecisions; each check decodes the field entries it needs. The only thing\nkept is the `.stats` component itself, opened once per snapshot.\n- **Decided per segment, on demand:** the reader keeps its query input\nand asks `can_match` for a segment when a search, count, or estimate is\nabout to open it. Nothing is decided or stored at open, so a planning\nreader that only estimates never reads statistics, and a parallel worker\ndecides only the segments it claims.\n- **Gated on partition columns:** the reader retains the query for\npruning only when a predicate names a `partition_by` column. Queries on\nother fields skip the statistics reads entirely and search every segment\nas before. This is a coarse first gate; widening it to any field with\nstatistics is follow-up work under #6078.\n- **One candidate rule:** search, lazy search, collection, counting,\ntop-k (including vector top-k), and candidate estimates all go through\nthe same per-segment decision. Lazy segment checkout stays lazy.\nCandidate document counts cap the row estimate in both partitioning\nmodes; candidate segment counts size DataFusion partitions only when\nthere are no range split points.\n- **Conservative answers:** term, range, existence, and Boolean\npredicates are supported. Unsupported shapes, incomparable values, NaN,\nand analyzed text keep the segment. Absent statistics keep the segment;\nan unreadable `.stats` component or entry aborts the query.\n- **Range partitions:** each partition classifies every segment against\nits range as fully included, partially included, or excluded, from the\nsegment's logical and empirical bounds. Fully included segments search\nthe base query without the partition filter; partially included segments\nkeep the filter; excluded segments are not opened. The classification is\ncomputed when a task is specialized and shown in `EXPLAIN` as\n`PgSearchScan … segments={partial=…, included=…, pruned=…}`. Omitting\nthe filter does not change scores: on a fully covered segment Tantivy's\nfast-field range already yields an `AllScorer`, which the Boolean weight\ndrops from the score. Non-partitioned scans report `segments=` as the\ncandidate count; Base Scan's `Segment Count` is unchanged. A dispatched\ntask carries the leader's classification (pruned segments listed by ID)\nand reuses it when it names exactly the worker's segment view, so\nworkers neither reopen statistics nor classify again; a mismatch under\nthe shared parallel state is an error, and a display copy classifies\nfrom its own snapshot. Both filter variants share one prepared base\nweight, so BM25 term statistics are gathered once per task rather than\nonce per variant.\n- **NULL partition keys:** the NULL clause uses `All AND NOT Exists`, as\nrequested in review, so NULL rows match and belong to partition zero.\nPartition scoring remains a separate discussion in #6344. A partition\ncovering every non-NULL value uses `Exists`, avoiding a range with two\nunbounded endpoints.\n- **Opaque conjuncts:** `and_query` receives a compiled Tantivy query it\ncannot inspect, so pruning keeps deciding from the original query input.\nThat is safe because a further AND never revives a rejected segment. Its\nproduction callers are the partitioned scan attaching its range and the\nhash-join IN-list pushdown. Proving the pushdown's values per segment is\n#6347.\n- **Collector validation:** `collect` now runs its own segment loop\ninstead of Tantivy's search, so it validates the collector against the\nschema itself, as Tantivy did, before any segment is visited.\n\nPostgreSQL planner costing remains follow-up work, marked `TODO(#6078)`\nat the two planning sites. Dynamic-filter updates belong to #6347.\n\nNote on the NULL clause cost: the corrected `All AND NOT Exists` clause\ndoes real work where the old pure-negative clause was dropped by\nTantivy, so partition 0 now walks every nullable segment outside its\nrange to find NULL keys. Measured at roughly 24 ns per document on a\n2M-row table. A cheaper \"docs without a value\" docset is follow-up work\nunder #6078.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Rust unit and property tests cover range, term, and Boolean answers\nagainst enumerated documents and `minimum_should_match` edge cases, with\nTantivy's two scorer paths as the oracle for single-clause Booleans.\n- Reader tests check scorer creation counts, statistics read only when a\ndecision needs them and not past an impossible first conjunct, a\nparallel checkout deciding only the segments it claims, lazy checkout,\nanalyzed-text and UUID fallbacks, injected read failures raising the\nquery error on both the routing and the pruning path, the partition\nclassification against an exact-query oracle (a nullable segment is\nfully included only by the NULL-owning partition), vector top-k honoring\nthe decision, collector validation with every segment pruned, and\nexecution after a segment merge.\n- Partition-bound execution tests check exact row IDs with no split,\nfinite and NULL splits, repeated boundaries, and scoring\nenabled/disabled.\n- `range_partition_null_keys` preserves all seven NULL-keyed rows of a\nLEFT JOIN in serial and MPP execution; the regression asserts a\nrange-partitioned Left join, `DistributedExec`, and launched workers.\n- Regress `partitioned_stats_pruning` asserts the per-partition\n`included`/`partial`/`pruned` counts a partitioned scan reports.\n- Statistics tests cover normalized text, timestamps, partitioned\nbuilds, and unpartitioned segments.\n- SQL regression coverage: `segment_pruning`,\n`partitioned_stats_pruning`, `recursive_estimates`, and\n`join_semi_anti`.\n\nLocal PG18 validation: 22 focused tests and 20 predicate unit/property\ntests passed. `segment_pruning`, `partitioned_stats_pruning`,\n`recursive_estimates`, and `range_partition_null_keys` matched expected\noutput. These edits were not tested locally on PG15–17.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Stu Hood <stuhood@gmail.com>",
