          "distinct": true,
          "id": "1b8c24684bd03093fcdfc4802023276d822b7861",
          "message": "perf: reuse the existing reader for serial aggregation (#6468)\n\n## What\n\nReuse the already-open index reader whenever aggregate execution falls\nback to a single local worker. Previously, the local worker opened\nanother reader over the same segments, repeating metadata setup and\nquery construction.\n\nBased directly on main. There is no GUC or count/query-shape gate, and\nno Tantivy dependency change.\n\n## Behavior\n\nThe local worker borrows the reader already opened for the current\nexecution. That reader has the same query, expression context, snapshot,\nand complete segment set. Reuse applies to counts, multiple aggregates,\ngrouping, heap filters, PostgreSQL expressions, and JSON aggregation\nrequests handled by this worker.\n\nThis is execution-local reuse, not a cache across statements or\nsnapshots. Parallel worker processes still open their own readers. The\nparallel leader also keeps its existing reader-opening path because it\nmust collect only its assigned segment subset; using the original full\nreader there would duplicate work.\n\n## Validation\n\n- PG18 release build and commit checks (formatting, Clippy, check, docs)\npassed.\n- 36 existing runtime tests passed: 24 aggregate custom-scan tests, 10\ncount/snapshot/mutation tests, and 2 JSON aggregation tests.\n- Additional local checks covered serial reuse with multiple aggregates,\nheap filters, correlated rescans, deletes/updates, and grouping before\nmutations. No new test is included in this PR.\n- A grouped count/sum query after deletes hits `PgList does not contain\npointers` on both this build and the previous runtime library; that\nexisting failure remains outside this change.\n- Restored the original local test runtime library after validation.",
          "timestamp": "2026-09-24T08:13:37-07:00",
          "tree_id": "0b8ae3060e7a58ffdaf9650f49c2c04f3e1345c8",
